  <h1>Types of Age Discrimination</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Disparate treatment</h2>
  <p>The most common ADEA claim: an employer intentionally treats an employee less favorably because of age. Examples: terminating a 58-year-old while retaining younger, less experienced employees; refusing to promote a 52-year-old while promoting 30-somethings with less tenure; reducing pay of older employees while maintaining pay for younger counterparts.</p>
  <h2>Disparate impact</h2>
  <p>A facially neutral policy that disproportionately harms workers 40 and older. Examples: requiring a specific educational credential that older workers are less likely to have, using algorithms in hiring that correlate with age proxies. Disparate impact claims under the ADEA were confirmed in <em>Smith v. City of Jackson</em>, 544 U.S. 228 (2005).</p>
  <h2>Age-based harassment</h2>
  <p>A pattern of age-related comments, jokes, or conduct that creates a hostile work environment. Must be severe or pervasive to meet the legal threshold. Isolated comments generally do not qualify, but a sustained pattern of derogatory age references can.</p>
  <h2>Retaliation</h2>
  <p>Adverse action against an employee who filed an ADEA complaint, reported age discrimination, or participated in an ADEA investigation. Retaliation claims can succeed even if the underlying age discrimination claim does not.</p>
  <h2>Constructive discharge</h2>
  <p>Making working conditions so intolerable that a reasonable person in the employee&rsquo;s position would feel compelled to resign. Often paired with an age discrimination claim when an employer tries to force out older workers without a direct termination.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-adea-damages-work">how ADEA damages work</a> and <a href="/guides/what-to-do-after-age-discrimination">what to do after age discrimination</a>.</p>
